/* data/learn-c/phy-11-08.js - GENERATED by tools/build_learn.py from data/learn/summaries/phy-11-08.json (our own words;
 * the notes of one chapter, loaded when the chapter is opened). Do not edit by hand. */
(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["phy-11-08"] = {"9.1":{"s":"Optics is the study of light and its behaviour. This chapter covers reflection and refraction at plane and curved surfaces, total internal reflection, lenses, prisms and dispersion, some natural phenomena like the rainbow, defects of lenses, and the working of common optical instruments such as the microscope and the telescope.","x":["Light is what lets us see. This chapter studies how light behaves when it meets mirrors, lenses and prisms, and how instruments such as the microscope and the telescope are built from them. It treats light as rays, which are straight lines along which light travels. This is called ray optics or geometrical optics.","The chapter covers the nature of light, reflection at plane and curved mirrors, refraction, total internal reflection, refraction at a spherical surface and in lenses, the dispersion of light by a prism, some natural phenomena such as the rainbow and the blue sky, the defects of lenses, and the optical instruments.","Study tip: ray optics is a topic with many formulas, so learn the sign convention well. Draw a ray diagram for every problem, and mark the object, the image, the focus and the centre of curvature."],"e":"A camera, a pair of spectacles, a microscope and a telescope all use lenses or mirrors. Each one forms an image by bending the rays of light, so that the image is in the right place and of the right size."},"9.2":{"s":"Light is the radiation that lets us see. Newton thought it was a stream of particles; Huygens treated it as a wave. Today we know it has both wave and particle properties. Light travels in straight lines in a uniform medium, which is why ray optics works for objects much larger than its wavelength, while effects like interference and diffraction need the wave picture.","x":["Light is a form of energy that gives us the sensation of sight. It travels in a straight line in a uniform medium, and its speed in vacuum is c = 3 × 10⁸ m/s, the greatest speed possible. Light does not need a medium. It reaches us from the Sun through empty space.","The nature of light has been described in different ways. Newton thought that it is a stream of tiny particles (the corpuscular theory), which explained reflection and the straight path. Huygens and others said that it is a wave, which explained interference, diffraction and polarisation. Maxwell showed that light is an electromagnetic wave. In the modern view, light shows both a wave nature and a particle nature, which is the dual nature of light.","The visible light has wavelengths from about 400 nm (violet) to 700 nm (red). Ray optics works well when the sizes of the objects and the openings are much larger than the wavelength, so that the effects of the wave nature can be ignored.","Common mistake: thinking that the speed of light is the same in all media. It is the greatest in vacuum, and smaller in a material."],"e":"Light takes about 8 minutes to reach the Earth from the Sun, since the distance is 1.5 × 10¹¹ m and the speed is 3 × 10⁸ m/s. The time is 500 s, which is about 8.3 minutes."},"9.3":{"s":"Ray optics treats light as rays that travel in straight lines. We use the Cartesian sign convention: distances are measured from the pole or optical centre, those along the incident light are positive and those against it negative, and heights above the principal axis are positive. A real object has a negative object distance; a real image from a mirror is also in front, so its distance is negative too.","w":["Apply the sign convention to every distance, including the focal length: the focal length of a concave mirror is negative."],"x":["Ray optics, or geometrical optics, treats light as rays, which are straight lines that show the direction in which light travels. A group of rays is called a beam. A beam may be parallel, diverging (spreading out) or converging (coming together).","Ray optics is based on a few simple laws: light travels in straight lines in a uniform medium, the laws of reflection, and the laws of refraction. Each ray travels independently, and the path of a ray can be reversed.","An object is a source of light rays. An image is formed where rays from a point of the object meet (a real image, which can be caught on a screen) or appear to meet (a virtual image, which cannot be caught on a screen). A real image is formed by a converging beam, and a virtual image is formed by a diverging beam.","Common mistake: drawing rays that bend in the wrong direction or that do not obey a law. For every ray, use only the rules for mirrors and lenses: a ray parallel to the axis, a ray through the centre, and a ray through the focus."],"e":"A cinema projector forms a real image of the film on the screen. A plane mirror forms a virtual image: you see an image behind the mirror, but no light actually goes there."},"9.4":{"s":"In reflection the angle of incidence equals the angle of reflection. A plane mirror gives a virtual, upright, same-size image as far behind as the object is in front. Two mirrors at an angle θ form several images. A curved mirror has a focal length equal to half the radius of curvature, and its mirror formula links object distance, image distance and focal length. A convex mirror always gives a virtual image.","f":[{"x":"n = 360/theta","n":"Plane mirrors at angle theta: n = 360/theta. n even: N = n - 1. n odd: N = n - 1 (object on the bisector) or n (elsewhere). n not whole: N = whole part of n.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 9.4, p.161"},{"x":"f = R/2","n":"Focal length of a spherical mirror; R = radius of curvature.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 9.4, p.162"},{"x":"1/f = 1/v + 1/u","n":"Mirror formula, with the Cartesian sign convention.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 9.4, p.162"},{"x":"P = 1/f","n":"Power of a mirror or lens in dioptre when f is in metres.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 9.4, p.162"}],"w":["Put u negative for a real object, and read the sign of v: negative means a real image in front of the mirror.","The formula holds for small apertures and rays close to the principal axis."],"x":["The laws of reflection: the angle of incidence equals the angle of reflection, and the incident ray, the reflected ray and the normal at the point of incidence are in the same plane. A plane mirror forms a virtual, erect image of the same size, as far behind the mirror as the object is in front. Two plane mirrors inclined at an angle θ form n = 360°/θ − 1 images, if 360°/θ is an even number.","For a spherical mirror, the focal length is f = R/2, where R is the radius of curvature. The mirror formula is 1/f = 1/v + 1/u, and the magnification is m = −v/u. Use the sign convention: distances are measured from the pole, and those in the direction of the incident light are positive. Then a concave mirror has a negative focal length in the Cartesian convention, and a real image has a negative v.","Rules for the ray diagrams: a ray parallel to the axis passes through the focus after reflection. A ray that goes through the focus comes out parallel to the axis. A ray that goes through the centre of curvature is reflected back along itself. For a concave mirror, an object beyond the centre gives a real, inverted, smaller image, and an object between the pole and the focus gives a virtual, erect, magnified image. A convex mirror always gives a virtual, erect, smaller image, so it is used as a rear-view mirror.","Common mistake: forgetting the signs. Check that the type of the image from the formula agrees with the ray diagram."],"e":"A concave mirror of f = 10 cm and an object at 30 cm. The real image is at 1/v = 1/10 − 1/30 = 1/15, so v = 15 cm. The magnification is v/u = 0.5, so the image is half the size, real, and inverted."},"9.5":{"s":"Refraction is the bending of light when it passes from one medium to another, because its speed changes. The absolute refractive index of a medium is the speed of light in vacuum divided by its speed in the medium; a denser medium has a larger index. The relative index of medium 2 with respect to medium 1 is the ratio of the indices. A pool looks shallower than it is.","f":[{"x":"n = c/v","n":"Absolute refractive index: c = speed of light in vacuum, v = speed in the medium.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 9.5, p.164"},{"x":"n_21 = n_2/n_1 = v_1/v_2","n":"Refractive index of medium 2 with respect to medium 1.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 9.5, p.164"},{"x":"n = real depth/apparent depth","n":"Seen from above (nearly normal viewing) through a plane surface of a medium of index n.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 9.5, p.165"}],"w":["The frequency of light does not change on refraction; speed and wavelength do.","Light bends towards the normal when it enters a denser medium."],"x":["Refraction is the bending of light when it goes from one medium to another, because its speed changes. The refractive index of a medium is n = c/v, the speed of light in vacuum divided by its speed in the medium. For water it is 1.33, for glass about 1.5. The medium with the larger n is optically denser.","Snell's law: n₁ sin i = n₂ sin r, where i and r are the angles of incidence and refraction, measured from the normal. The incident ray, the refracted ray and the normal are in one plane. Light bends towards the normal when it goes into a denser medium, and away from the normal when it goes into a rarer one. The relative refractive index of medium 2 with respect to medium 1 is n₂₁ = n₂/n₁ = v₁/v₂. The frequency does not change, but the speed and the wavelength do.","Apparent depth: when we look from above into water, almost vertically, the bottom seems to be raised. The apparent depth = real depth/n. A pond of 12 cm depth, with n = 4/3, seems to be 9 cm deep. The bending of a stick at the water surface, and the twinkling of stars, are also effects of refraction.","Common mistake: measuring the angles from the surface and not from the normal."],"e":"Light goes from air to glass (n = 1.5), with an angle of incidence of 30°. sin r = sin 30°/1.5 = 0.333, so r = 19.5°. The ray bends towards the normal. The speed of light in the glass is 3 × 10⁸/1.5 = 2 × 10⁸ m/s."},"9.6":{"s":"When light goes from a denser to a rarer medium, it bends away from the normal. At the critical angle of incidence the refracted ray grazes the surface at 90°. For larger angles the light is totally reflected back, which is total internal reflection. It needs light to travel from the denser to the rarer medium and the angle to exceed the critical angle. It is used in optical fibres, prism binoculars, periscopes and in the sparkle of diamonds.","f":[{"x":"sin(i_c) = 1/n","n":"Critical angle for light going from a medium of refractive index n into air.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 9.6, p.166"}],"w":["Total internal reflection is impossible from rarer to denser: the light must start in the denser medium.","A larger refractive index gives a smaller critical angle."],"x":["When light goes from a denser medium to a rarer one, it bends away from the normal. As the angle of incidence is increased, the angle of refraction increases faster, until it is 90° for a particular angle of incidence, the critical angle i_c. For angles of incidence greater than i_c, there is no refracted ray, and all the light is reflected back into the denser medium. This is total internal reflection.","The conditions: the light must go from a denser to a rarer medium, and the angle of incidence must be greater than the critical angle. The critical angle is given by sin i_c = 1/n for a medium in air. For glass with n = 1.5, i_c = 41.8°. For water, n = 1.33 and i_c = 48.6°. For diamond, n = 2.42 and i_c = 24.4°.","Applications: the sparkle of a diamond (the small critical angle means that most light is reflected many times inside), right-angled prisms in binoculars and periscopes, optical fibres, which carry light signals and are used in communication and in medicine, and the mirage in a desert.","Common mistake: using the formula sin i_c = n, or applying total internal reflection when light goes from a rarer to a denser medium. It cannot happen in that direction."],"e":"A ray in glass (n = 1.5) hits the glass-air surface at 45°. Since 45° is greater than the critical angle 41.8°, the ray is totally reflected. At 30°, part of the light is refracted out."},"9.7":{"s":"Refraction at a spherical surface connects the object distance, image distance, radius of curvature and the two refractive indices. A thin lens is two such surfaces. Its focal length depends on the refractive index of the glass and the radii of curvature (lens maker's equation) and on the surrounding medium. Thin lenses in contact add their powers. A convex lens converges light and has positive focal length; a concave lens diverges it and has negative focal length.","f":[{"x":"n_2/v - n_1/u = (n_2 - n_1)/R","n":"Refraction at a single spherical surface: light goes from medium n_1 to medium n_2.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 9.7, p.169"},{"x":"1/f = (n - 1)*(1/R_1 - 1/R_2)","n":"Lens maker's equation for a thin lens of index n in air.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 9.7, p.170"},{"x":"1/f = 1/v - 1/u","n":"Thin lens formula (the book's form, with the Cartesian sign convention).","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 9.7, p.168"},{"x":"P = P_1 + P_2, 1/f = 1/f_1 + 1/f_2","n":"Two thin lenses in contact: powers add (in dioptre).","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 9.7, p.168"},{"x":"f = R","n":"Symmetric double convex lens of index 1.5 (R_1 = R, R_2 = -R).","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 9.7, p.170"}],"w":["Use the sign convention for both radii: R₁ is positive and R₂ negative for a double convex lens.","Focal length of a lens changes with the medium: a lens of lower index than its surroundings acts the opposite way."],"x":["For refraction at a spherical surface of radius R, between the media of the refractive indices n₁ (where the light comes from) and n₂, the relation, for rays close to the axis, is n₂/v − n₁/u = (n₂ − n₁)/R. It is used with the Cartesian sign convention, and with light going in the positive direction.","A thin lens has two such surfaces. For a lens of refractive index n in air, the lens maker's formula is 1/f = (n − 1)(1/R₁ − 1/R₂). The lens formula is 1/f = 1/v − 1/u, and the magnification is m = v/u. A convex lens (converging) has a positive focal length, and a concave lens (diverging) has a negative one. The power of a lens is P = 1/f, with f in metres, in dioptres.","A convex lens forms a real, inverted image for an object beyond its focus, and a virtual, erect, magnified image for an object within the focal length (a magnifying glass). An object at 2f gives an image of the same size at 2f. A concave lens always gives a virtual, erect, smaller image.","Common mistake: using the wrong signs of R₁ and R₂. For a double convex lens, R₁ is positive and R₂ is negative. Another mistake: using the mirror formula, with a plus sign, for a lens."],"e":"A lens with n = 1.5, R₁ = +15 cm and R₂ = −30 cm: 1/f = 0.5 × (1/15 + 1/30) = 0.05, so f = 20 cm. An object 30 cm away gives 1/v = 1/20 − 1/30 = 1/60, so v = 60 cm, real, and the image is twice as large."},"9.8":{"s":"A prism bends light by refraction at its two faces, and the ray deviates towards the base. The angle of deviation first falls and then rises as the angle of incidence increases; at minimum deviation the ray passes symmetrically. Different colours bend by different amounts, which splits white light into a spectrum: dispersion. Violet bends most and red least. A thin prism of small angle bends light by an angle proportional to (n − 1).","f":[{"x":"r_1 + r_2 = A","n":"Prism: A = angle of the prism, r_1 and r_2 = the angles of refraction at the two faces.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 9.8, p.172"},{"x":"i + e = A + delta","n":"Angle of deviation delta in terms of the angle of incidence i and emergence e.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 9.8, p.172"},{"x":"n = sin((A + delta_m)/2)/sin(A/2)","n":"Prism formula: the minimum deviation angle is written with a subscript m.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 9.8, p.173"},{"x":"delta = A*(n - 1)","n":"Thin prism (A less than about 10 degrees).","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 9.8, p.174"},{"x":"delta_V - delta_R = A*(n_V - n_R)","n":"Angular dispersion between violet and red.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 9.8, p.174"}],"w":["At minimum deviation i = e and r₁ = r₂ = A/2.","Violet has the larger refractive index and is deviated most."],"x":["When white light passes through a prism, it is split into the colours violet, indigo, blue, green, yellow, orange and red. This is dispersion. It happens because the refractive index of glass is different for different colours: it is the highest for violet (the shortest wavelength), and the lowest for red. So violet is bent the most, and red the least.","For a ray through a prism of angle A: the angles inside are related by r₁ + r₂ = A, and the angle of deviation is δ = i + e − A, where i and e are the angles of incidence and emergence. The deviation is the least for a symmetrical passage, when i = e. This is the minimum deviation δ_m. Then r = A/2, and n = sin((A + δ_m)/2)/sin(A/2).","A graph of the deviation against the angle of incidence is a curve with one minimum. For a small prism angle and small angle of incidence, δ = (n − 1)A. The dispersive power measures how much the prism spreads the colours.","Common mistake: thinking that the prism adds the colours to light. White light is a mixture of the colours, and the prism only separates them."],"e":"A prism of A = 60° and n = 1.5. At minimum deviation, r = 30°, sin i = 1.5 × 0.5 = 0.75, so i = 48.6°. δ_m = 2i − A = 97.2° − 60° = 37.2°."},"9.9":{"s":"Sunlight produces several natural phenomena. A rainbow forms when sunlight is refracted, totally reflected inside raindrops and refracted again, spreading the colours. In the primary rainbow there is one internal reflection and red is on the outer edge; in the secondary rainbow there are two reflections and the order of colours is reversed. The Sun looks reddish at sunrise and sunset, and the sky looks blue, because of scattering of light by air molecules.","x":["Several beautiful sights in nature come from the refraction, reflection and scattering of sunlight. A rainbow is formed when sunlight is refracted and dispersed on entering a drop of water, reflected once inside it, and refracted again on leaving. The red light comes out at a smaller angle, so red is at the outer edge and violet at the inner edge of the primary rainbow. You must have the Sun behind you and the rain in front.","The blue colour of the sky is due to the scattering of sunlight by the molecules of air. The scattering is stronger for shorter wavelengths, in proportion to 1/λ⁴ (Rayleigh scattering). So blue is scattered about five times as much as red, and we see blue from all directions. At sunrise and sunset, the light goes through a long path in the air, the blue is scattered out of the beam, and the Sun looks red.","Other phenomena: a mirage, when total internal reflection happens in the layers of hot air over a road or a desert, so the road seems to have water on it. The twinkling of the stars is due to the changing refraction of the starlight in the moving layers of the atmosphere, and the Sun is visible a little before the sunrise by atmospheric refraction.","Common mistake: thinking that the sky is blue because of the reflection of the sea. It is due to scattering. Also, red is used for danger signals because it is scattered the least, so it can be seen from far away."],"e":"Why is the Sun red at sunset? The light goes through a thicker layer of air. The short blue wavelengths are scattered away from the beam, and mainly the red and orange reach our eyes."},"9.10":{"s":"Real lenses and mirrors do not form perfect images. Spherical aberration occurs because rays far from the axis focus at a different point from rays near it; it is reduced by using a plano-convex lens with the curved face towards the incident rays, or a parabolic mirror. Chromatic aberration occurs because a lens bends each colour differently; it is reduced by an achromatic combination of a convex and a concave lens of different glasses.","w":["Chromatic aberration exists only for lenses (prisms disperse); mirrors do not show it because reflection does not depend on colour."],"x":["Real lenses and mirrors do not form perfect images. The faults are called aberrations. Spherical aberration happens because rays that fall near the edge of a spherical lens or mirror are focused at a different point from rays that fall near the axis, so the image is blurred. It is reduced by using a stop to cut the outer rays, by using a lens that has the right shape, or by using a parabolic mirror, as in a telescope or a car headlight.","Chromatic aberration happens because the refractive index, and so the focal length of a lens, is different for the different colours. The violet is focused nearer to the lens than the red, so the image has coloured edges. It is reduced by using a combination of two lenses of different glasses, a convex and a concave one, called an achromatic doublet.","A mirror has no chromatic aberration, because reflection does not depend on the wavelength. This is one reason why large astronomical telescopes use mirrors. Lenses also have other faults, such as distortion and astigmatism, which are corrected in good cameras with several lenses.","Common mistake: thinking that a mirror has chromatic aberration. It does not, but it can have spherical aberration."],"e":"A simple convex lens makes a white object look as if it had a coloured fringe, red on one side and blue on the other. This is chromatic aberration. The lens of a good camera has several parts to remove it."},"9.11":{"s":"Optical instruments make objects appear larger by increasing the angle they subtend at the eye. Magnifying power is the ratio of the angle subtended with the instrument to the angle subtended by the object at the least distance of distinct vision, D = 25 cm. A simple microscope is one convex lens. A compound microscope has an objective and an eyepiece, and its magnification is the product of the two. A telescope has a large objective and a small eyepiece.","f":[{"x":"M = beta/alpha = D/u","n":"Angular magnification of a simple microscope: D = 25 cm, u = object distance.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 9.11, p.181"},{"x":"M_max = 1 + D/f, M_min = D/f","n":"Simple microscope: image at the near point (D) gives the maximum, image at infinity the minimum.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 9.11, p.181"},{"x":"M = m_o*M_e","n":"Compound microscope: lateral magnification of the objective times angular magnification of the eyepiece.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 9.11, p.182"},{"x":"M = f_o/f_e","n":"Astronomical telescope in normal adjustment (final image at infinity).","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 9.11, p.183"},{"x":"L = f_o + f_e","n":"Length of the telescope in normal adjustment.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 9.11, p.183"}],"w":["Normal adjustment of a telescope means the final image is at infinity, so the lenses are f₀ + fₑ apart.","For a compound microscope, the objective has the SMALLER focal length of the two lenses."],"x":["A simple microscope is a convex lens of a short focal length. The object is kept within the focal length, and a virtual, erect, magnified image is formed. The magnifying power is the ratio of the angle that the image makes at the eye to the angle of the object at the near point D = 25 cm. For the image at the near point, M = 1 + D/f, and for the image at infinity, M = D/f.","A compound microscope has two lenses: an objective of a short focal length, which forms a real, magnified image of the object, and an eyepiece, which acts as a simple microscope for this image. The total magnification is the product M = m_o × M_e, where m_o = v_o/u_o is the magnification of the objective. It can be several hundred times.","A refracting telescope is used to see distant objects. The objective, with a long focal length, forms a real image of the object at its focus, and the eyepiece, with a short focal length, magnifies it. In normal adjustment, the final image is at infinity, the length is f_o + f_e, and the magnifying power is M = f_o/f_e. A larger objective gathers more light. A reflecting telescope uses a mirror as the objective.","Common mistake: confusing the formulas of the microscope and the telescope. For the microscope, the magnifications are multiplied. For the telescope, the focal lengths are in a ratio."],"e":"A magnifying glass of f = 5 cm used with the image at 25 cm gives M = 1 + 25/5 = 6. A telescope with f_o = 100 cm and f_e = 2 cm in normal adjustment has M = 50, and its length is 102 cm."}};
